  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Above normal temperatures and dry conditions \nwill prevail through Friday. \n\nWarm and dry conditions continue through Friday as a ridge of high \npressure slides from west to east across the southeast Region. At \nthe surface, dry high pressure will be weakening before moving into \nthe nearshore Atlantic waters on Thursday into Friday. With h925 \ntemperatures peaking in the 20-22 deg C range both today and \nThursday, which is in the mid to upper 90th percentile for the \ndates, near-record to record high temperatures will be possible on \nboth days. Areas away from the coast will peak in the mid to upper \n80s, with lower to mid 80s along the coast, with the individual \nrecords at our climate sites in the climate section at the bottom.\n\nSimilar to what we saw earlier this morning, patchy shallow ground \nfog will be possible Thursday morning. Maximum dew points today \nshould surge into the lower to upper 60s, and with overnight lows \nsimilarly in the lower to upper 60s, cross over temperatures \nare expected to be reached. However, with high condensation \npressure deficits, the fog should be patchy and shallow. Areas \nin the vicinity of Darien, GA have the highest probabilities for\nvisibilities below 1 mile near 25%. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Expect gusty winds and temperatures well below \nnormal this weekend behind a strong cold front.\n\nA strong cold front will sweep across southeast Georgia and the SC \nLowcountry late Friday night into Saturday morning. NAM12 shows a \nfrontal wave move north along the coast Saturday morning, resulting \nin a band of light of rainfall across the forecast area, GFS and \nECMWF along with the AI variants generally agrees that it should be \na dry cold front, only sprinkles across northern Dorchester and \nBerkeley Counties areas early Saturday morning. GFS H85 temperatures \nover Lake Moultrie cools by 10 degrees between 0Z Sat to 0Z Sun. As \na result, temperature will fall well below normal on Sat, with highs \nranging around 70 degrees. In addition, north-northeast winds across \nthe region will be breezy, with gusts around 25 mph. In fact, north \nwinds across Lake Moultrie may strengthen early Saturday, gusting \naround 25 kts. A Lake Wind Advisory may be needed beginning early \nSaturday morning.\n\nSaturday night into Sunday, the center of a 1035 mb high will settle \nover VA by daybreak Sunday. The sfc high will ridge SW across the \nCarolinas and Georgia on Sunday. Northeast winds will continue \nthrough the period, generally between 10-15 mph with gusts between \n20 to 25 mph. The northeast winds will maintain cool llvl \nthicknesses across the forecast area. Using a blend of guidance, low \ntemperatures on Sunday are forecast to range from the upper 40s \ninland to the mid to upper 50s along the coast. High temperatures \nare forecast to range from the mid to upper 60s across SE GA and \nthe SC Lowcountry. \n\nThe center of sfc high pressure should shift over off the New \nEngland coast on Monday, resulting in winds to weaken and shift from \nthe east-northeast. Temperatures across the CWA should warm near \nseasonable normals, mid to upper 70s. Gradual warming expected \nthrough mid-week.",
